3. TOP 5 DEEP DIVES

#1 Agentinel =E2=80=94 capability risk is compositional

The important mechanism is not another prompt scanner. Agentinel
classifies tools into untrusted-input, sensitive-access and
external-effect axes, then evaluates the combined fleet. A user can
install three individually acceptable servers and accidentally
assemble the complete exfiltration path. That maps directly to
Privately=E2=80=99s authority graph.

6m inference: fleet-level MCP composition checks become common. 18m:
policy engines reason about reachable action chains rather than tool
permissions. 36m: capability graph compilation becomes an admission
step for agent environments.

Smallest experiment: give QP three benign synthetic tools where no
tool individually spans the lethal trifecta but their composition
does. Metric: QP identifies the cross-tool path before execution.
Falsifier: existing per-tool grants already prevent every chain
without composition analysis.

#2 AgentOpt / self-improving-agent-harness =E2=80=94 self-improvement is
becoming ordinary eval engineering

Both projects turn RSI into a tractable loop: mutate a bounded
artifact, run fixed evals, preserve only measured wins, retain
rollback. This is much more immediately useful than grand
recursive-intelligence claims.

Privately implication: QP processors themselves can be autoresearched,
provided the security invariant/eval suite is immutable to the
optimizer. The thing that must be protected is the evaluator and
authority boundary, not the optimizer.

Smallest experiment: allow outer agent to mutate only a policy/routing
module against a fixed 30-case authority suite. Metric: improvement
without any forbidden-action regression.

#3 Test-time-search attacker

The Sep 3 paper treats indirect injection as an adaptive search
problem. Defenses that look robust against a fixed payload set can
fail as attacker search budget rises. This suggests security
benchmarks need an x-axis for adversarial compute.

Privately probe: plot attack success vs attacker tokens/rounds against
the same QP policy. Metric: area under attack-success-vs-budget curve
rather than single ASR.

#4 nilbox =E2=80=94 enforcement below the agent

rednakta/nilbox runs agents/MCP servers in a dedicated VM, removes
real credentials from the guest entirely, and enforces host-side
default-deny egress with credential injection only at trusted
destinations. This is much closer to a durable security primitive than
asking the model not to leak secrets.
https://github.com/rednakta/nilbox

Privately implication: pair semantic authority/QP with an
uncooperative lower enforcement layer. The agent should never possess
the secret it is forbidden to exfiltrate.

#5 video-agent-skills =E2=80=94 media production becoming an agent team

chenyuxiaojin/video-agent-skills coordinates 11 Claude Code skills
from topic through
research/script/storyboard/voice/assets/timeline/platform metadata and
emits DaVinci or CapCut-ready projects. It is a strong example of
media automation moving from one giant generator into inspectable
specialist-agent pipelines.
https://github.com/chenyuxiaojin/video-agent-skills

